    "Namespace string: it does not shout -- it takes the generic path")

-- Section 4/5/6 coverage: operators, assignments and special characters.
-- A lone operator ("the `+` operator") cannot be parsed by {.rexx}, so it is
-- published by element name. By default FencedCode uses operator=/special=/
-- assignment="group", so a mention emits the generic tag (rx-op / rx-spe / rx-asg);
-- the specific token appears only when the document asks for detail/full.

::Method test_prose_operator_mention
  -- Default operator=group: the generic "rx-op" tag, not the specific token.
  result = FencedCode("test.md", .resources~ProseOperatorPlus)
  flat   = result~makeString('L', "0A"x)
  self~assertTrue(flat~pos('<code class="rexx rx-op">+</code>') > 0, -
    "Prose mention: rexx-plus resolves to the generic 'rx-op' under operator=group")

